  "articleBody": "It's tempting to want to put a fresh coat of paint on a foreclosed property right away – it feels like a quick win and can make a huge visual difference! But hold on, painting too early is one of the biggest mistakes you can make. Think of paint as the final touch, not a band-aid. If you paint over water stains, mildew, cracks, or active leaks, you're not fixing the problem; you're just covering it up. That trapped moisture can lead to bigger issues like mold or rot, and those cracks will reappear. The proper sequence is critical: address structural issues, roof integrity, electrical, plumbing, and moisture protection *before* you even pick up a paintbrush. Only once the underlying structure is sound, and all necessary repairs to drywall and other surfaces are completed and dried, should you proceed with priming and painting. This ensures your paint job lasts and truly enhances the property rather than hiding deeper problems. If you're unsure about the repair sequence, a professional like HH Remodeling LLC can guide you.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}